17. Chart rendering specifications
Charts are particularly important in this interface.

There are five distinct analytical chart types.

They should not all use the same generic chart template.
Section	Chart type	Rendering approach
2	Line chart	SVG
3	Stacked bar chart	SVG or Canvas
5	Mixed bar and line chart	SVG or Canvas
7	Two-series comparison chart	SVG
4	Progress indicator	CSS or SVG

17.1 Common chart appearance
All charts share these properties:
- Transparent background.
- No white chart panels.
- Thin muted axes.
- Subtle grid lines.
- Small axis labels.
- Minimal chart borders.
- Carefully positioned legends.
- Colored series markers.
Avoid using default chart-library styling.

Default chart legends, white backgrounds, thick axes, and large tooltips would make the result look significantly different from the reference.
17.2 SVG chart example
A reusable chart renderer should calculate:
- X-coordinate based on data index or date.
- Y-coordinate based on the numerical value.
- Grid line positions.
- Axis labels.
- SVG path coordinates.
- Marker positions.
The green line should be rendered as an SVG path.

For example:
function createLinePath(points) {
    return points.map((point, index) => {
        const command = index === 0 ? "M" : "L";

        return `${command} ${point.x} ${point.y}`;
    }).join(" ");
}


Then:
<svg viewBox="0 0 800 130">
    <path
        d="M 20 80 L 90 70 L 160 63"
        fill="none"
        stroke="#35E887"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
    />
</svg>


For smoother line charts, use cubic Bézier interpolation.

For the stacked bars, use individual <rect> elements.

For the water balance chart, draw the bars first and the line series afterward so that the lines remain visible.

